WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:09.080
So I'm going to talk about the updated rRNA
databases.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:14.500
You can use these for identification and phylogenetic
analysis of fungi, bacteria and archea, and

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:21.990
as I said a minute ago Barbara Robbertse is
here and I'm Peter Cooper I'm doing this presentation

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:26.490
even though I'm not a subject matter expert
on this.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.720
So this is the outline were talking today.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:40.039
Talking a little bit about what target loci
are and then the background on structure of

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:43.350
Ribosomal RNA genes.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:49.910
The scope and content of the NCBI RefSeq Ribosomal
RNA projects, which include the 16S bacterial

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:57.949
and archaeal, the fungal ITS, which is the
largest one but we also have large subunit

00:00:57.949 --> 00:01:00.329
and a small subunit database.

00:01:00.329 --> 00:01:08.060
We will talk about retrieving the data and
we will do some live demonstrations.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:12.000
So we talk about things at NCBI called targeted
loci.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.360
These are marker genes, barcodes, or measuring
tape.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:27.480
Used for phylogenetic analysis or taxon identification.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.649
This is molecular identification as opposed
to the traditional morphological means like

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:38.890
a colony on a plate.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.859
Examples of these kinds of sequences are Cytochrome
oxidase Park consequences.

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:45.380
Various protein coding genes.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:52.009
And the mitochondrial D loop, and ones going
to talk about today which are the structural

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:53.500
RNA gene.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.979
For prokaryotes there are the 16S, 23S and
5S.

00:01:55.979 --> 00:02:04.350
For eukaryotes there is an 18S, 5.8S and a
28 or 25S depending on the group of organisms,

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:10.009
and a special kind of sequence that's more
variable, it is between the 5.8S and those

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:13.860
other two subunits called the internal transcribed
spacer regions.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:19.650
ITS1 and ITS2 and also a 5S rRNA gene.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:24.930
We make sequences for these and we call them
reference targeted loci.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.550
We've got records available from RefSeq.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.150
And the point I want to make today is that
there are BLAST databases we have made for

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.560
you to use.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:41.510
These rRNA genes are from microbes in the
traditional sense of that word in the old-fashioned

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:46.310
sense which include the Prokaryotes, Archaeal
and bacteria.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:52.900
As well as fungi . The good thing about these
sequences that we produce is that they are

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:59.100
associated for the most part with taxonomic
type material which is material or a specimen

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:03.700
or culture that a new species description
is based on.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.370
This ensures that the correct name is tied
with the sequence.

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:13.709
That's been a big problem with sequences going
into GenBank where the identity might be incorrect.

00:03:13.709 --> 00:03:16.450
What are these are useful.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:20.129
Confirming organism by identification.

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:25.231
If you've got an unknown they are good for
assigning a preliminary ID or placement in

00:03:25.231 --> 00:03:26.231
a taxonomic group.

00:03:26.231 --> 00:03:30.519
You're not necessarily going to be to identify
the species and will talk more about that.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:32.850
You can use these to construct polygenic trees.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.390
Or to put them in your trees to get anchor
points.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:50.840
Let's talk a little bit about the structure
of the rRNA genes in Prokaryotes and eukaryotes.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.130
Here's an example using our sequence viewer.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:57.920
We'll look at the ribosomal cassette.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.430
There are multiple of these in bacteria.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:09.950
This is E. coli with seven copies and a 16
S gene shown on the left hand side and that's

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:13.219
the one with got a corresponding record were
talking about today.

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:16.260
This is the RefSeq RNA for this 16S, NR_074891.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:21.030
This is showing the BLAST alignment for that
part of the E. coli genome.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.060
Here's the 23 S and 5S.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.900
These are the main reagents we'll be talking
about today for bacteria.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.900
And these are genes for a fungus.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:41.000
This is a yeast, Kazachstania africana, you
can see the structure of the RNA cassette

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.000
there.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:50.520
This is the 5 S the 18S and the 25S large
subunit RNA.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:58.990
This guy right here, the 5.8, there is a space
between 18 and 25, this is the internal transcribed

00:04:58.990 --> 00:04:59.990
spacer regions.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:00.990
There's 2.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:01.990
One on each side.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.900
These are more variable than the conserved
genes themselves.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:15.250
And we make a sequence in this spacer RefSeq
that includes that 5.8 but also some of the

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:19.640
flank on either side so it includes that ITS1
and the ITS 2 sequence .

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:25.930
That's a very useful sequence use for identification
and phylogenetic analysis.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:32.780
So little bit more about these Targeted loci
data sets that we have.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.830
One way to access them is to the BioProject
site.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:39.880
There is a number of these and I've highlighted
the ones were talking about today.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:46.820
16 S for bacteria and archaea, the fungi,
internal transcribed spacer region the ITS,

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:50.520
28 S and 18 S. Those are the BioProject IDs.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:56.340
These are all a part of these targeted Loci
projects that we have it NCBI.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:01.960
What's good about these BioProject identifiers
is they are useful for retrieving these sets

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:08.330
of sequences from our Entrez system and I'll
show you that in a bit.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:13.380
Looking at what kind of organisms and what
kinds of taxonomic distribution we have for

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:19.150
the organisms in the group for 16 S, this
is a breakdown of what groups are represented

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.300
and what I have done here is used a name that
is sort of a handle for group of bacteria.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.050
These are the so-called BLAST names we have
in our taxonomy database.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:31.300
So these are groups that most people recognize.

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:37.530
The highest most represented group are the
high GC positive bacteria and there are a

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.650
number of other organisms represented here.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:45.479
We've got a broad spectrum of things and we
can talk later on.

00:06:45.479 --> 00:06:51.090
This is a tiny bit of the microbial diversity
that's out there.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:58.860
There's 21,333 of these records, 20,000 of
them for bacteria and 979 for Archaeal and

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:03.700
it's about 16,000 species at this point.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:15.050
The fungal database is smaller there's 11,751
records and 11,665 species represented.

00:07:15.050 --> 00:07:20.699
This is a breakdown of the ITS fungal here,
also the 28S and 18S.

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:23.930
They are slightly smaller databases.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:30.759
And these are BLAST names, not necessarily
names that fungal taxonomists would like to

00:07:30.759 --> 00:07:31.759
use.

00:07:31.759 --> 00:07:36.650
Barbara did not like them very much but they
are good handles for groups of fungi.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:44.960
This is a better breakdown of the taxonomic
coverage basically by showing the number of

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:53.340
records at the various levels, phylum, class,
order, genus and species for the small subunit

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.210
the ITS and the large subunit.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:02.080
Barbara made the slide it's very nice and
shows you the number in the various groups

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:09.360
so for Ascomycota there's a large group with
8,492 records and so on down to the species

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.699
level.

00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:16.180
And in terms of phylogenetic analysis a lot
of times people want to use combinations of

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:17.180
genes.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:23.580
The table at the bottom of the slide shows
you what we have for a particular taxon.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:27.979
So the last line is the one that we have everything
for.

00:08:27.979 --> 00:08:31.680
The small subunit, ITS and the large subunit.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:42.970
So that's 1400 2 taxa for that and she shows
you those taxa that are the most represented.

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:47.350
And there is coverage breakdown by phylum,
class, order family and genus.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:55.010
These are useful if you want to use both or
as many of the genes in a phylogenetic tree.

00:08:55.010 --> 00:09:00.350
You can get those from the RefSeq FTP site.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:05.280
If you're looking at one of these records.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.010
This is the flat file of one of these records
to show you the kind of information that is

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:11.010
on here.

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:15.010
You can see these in our Entrez system.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:22.410
First of all, the definition line is controlled
by the RefSeq curators.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:27.080
The organism information will be updated with
the correct organism and what happens is a

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:32.040
lot of times with the GenBank record is the
definition line organism will be out of date

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:35.860
even though taxonomy is updated in the organism
field.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:41.090
The important thing about the def line when
we talk about the blast databases is that

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:43.670
this is the thing that shows up in the blast
description.

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:49.390
A lot of people are looking at the species
name so it's useful for these RefSeq records

00:09:49.390 --> 00:09:53.150
to ensure that the species name is the best
when we can come up with.

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:57.260
Here's a link to the RefSeq BioProject.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:04.310
Here's a link to the original Genbank or record
that was used to make this record.

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:10.460
What's nice here is that you can go back to
BioCollections and look at the culture collection

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:16.530
and you can link to the outside culture collection
directly, e.g., ATCC, and see about how you

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:20.200
would order this culture to verify it yourself.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:28.150
This is a 16S record this is for Lactobacillus
delbrueckii, and there's the RefSeq accession

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:30.280
number here, NR_188558.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:38.410
Here's the corresponding record for one of
the ITS sequences for fungus, Trichophyton

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:39.810
rubrum.

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:46.400
You have the curated def line the BioProject
link and the link to the outside culture collection,

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:51.140
CBS, where you can order this particular culture
if you wanted to verify and work on it yourself.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:59.230
So let's talk about how you can get access
to these data.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:02.360
You can get them directly from our Entrez
Nucleotide system.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:06.980
It's easy to download these because they are
not huge sets of sequences.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:12.810
You can use these queries, 33175[bioproject],
the BioProject number followed by the square-bracketed

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:22.050
field, in the Nucleotide system to get the
sets, then use the send to menu to download

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:24.060
these.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:30.560
If you want the set in handy file you can
go to our RefSeq FTP site,

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.580
https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/refseq/TargetedLoci,
the URL is given here there is a targetedloci

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:35.750
directory that has access to the bacteria,
archaea

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:36.820
and the fungal sequences.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:46.400
And the other thing I want to mention and
one of the things we want to promote is that

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:49.340
you can get these as blast databases.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:55.480
They are also available on the web so that's
what we're using for our examples.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:00.350
They are on the blast FTP site in the blast
db directory, https://ftp.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/blast/db.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:05.130
You can get the ones were talking about today,
the 16S, 18S, 28S and the ITS.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.410
These are the RefSeq databases.

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:15.330
I will point out that there are sequences
there that we have gleaned from GenBank for

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:17.600
these particular regions for other organizations.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:22.230
I'm not going to talk about those databases
today but you're welcome to check them out.

00:12:22.230 --> 00:12:29.110
One cool thing bout the blast databases now
is we've got these version 5 databases.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:34.830
The taxonomy information it's baked in to
them and you can use switches in the blastdbcmd

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:41.150
program to dump out various things, what organisms
are in there, what the accession numbers are

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:42.680
and all these type of things.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:45.180
That's very handy.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:50.001
The other thing that's nice about getting
a blast databases even if you don't run a

00:12:50.001 --> 00:12:56.300
blast against them directly you can use them
as a way of dumping out sequences in various

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:57.320
formats.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:01.140
This is a nice way to get the FASTA to use
in other applications.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:05.990
And one thing I should point out is those
databases are non-redundant.

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:12.300
So there might be cases where the sequence
for different species is the same they will

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:17.790
be collapsed into a single FASTA but you'll
still be able to see the two titles in the

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:18.810
blast database.

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:23.960
If you really want to have every FASTA sequence
you want to use the RefSeq download if you

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:29.420
want all the records instead of the non-redundant
set that is part of blast.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:36.520
So let me point out a few things that are
important to realize when using these types

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:44.110
of targeted loci, an important one I alluded
to earlier is that this is limited coverage

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.050
of the taxonomic representatio.

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:55.730
So if you just do a quick count of the species
level nodes at NCBI these are often things

00:13:55.730 --> 00:14:00.960
that don't have formal names just things that
are at the end of the taxonomy classification.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:06.790
There's 450,000 bacteria and 150,000 fungi.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:13.140
For bacteria we've got only 16,000 species
in the 16S database and for fungi there's

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:16.450
only 12,000 species in the ITS database.

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:21.920
Who knows how many species of microbes there
are on the planet.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:28.230
I've seen estimates upwards of 10 to the 30th
species so this is just the tip of the iceberg

00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.980
in terms of the diversity.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:36.630
If you got an unknown sequence, there may
not be the corresponding species in these

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:37.630
databases.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:43.620
And just using 16S and ITS alone, there might
be a lot more species out there.

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:48.810
There's going to be some species that are
not separable based on the ITS or 16S alone

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:56.420
you need to use a multi-gene analysis to get
accurate identification and to get phylogenetic

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:58.770
trees.

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:04.630
The technique were talking about today that
searches these is blast and if hyou go to

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:11.810
the blast page now you have a separate radio
button that you can click and you have access

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:17.810
to these databases from a pulldown list.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:19.880
You can restrict to only those from type material.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:22.740
And most are from type material.

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.690
That's all very good.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:31.290
Keep in mind and we'll see examples of this
in a few minutes that there's some limitations

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:32.640
that BLAST has.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:38.230
It sorts by e-value and alignment score and
not by the percednt identity.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:43.270
This causes some things to happen that might
be confusing at first.

00:15:43.270 --> 00:15:48.560
Your longer sequence that is a worse match
might appear higher in the results so take

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:53.930
a look at both query coverage and the percentage
identity to help identify things.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:59.450
Also BLAST does not use position specific
information in the alignment.

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:06.530
So every position gets equal weight and in
reality some are more informative than others.

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:12.650
Look at how the match fits in to a tree with
the results to get a better idea of the placement.

00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:17.880
You might need to build trees using real multiple
sequence alignments and tree building with

00:16:17.880 --> 00:16:23.720
multiple genes to get an accurate view of
things.

