the opposite strand there's 16 S, 20 3S and

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:20.490
5 S and here's our perfect match to this portion
of the chromosome.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:27.530
This is a case where we hit two different
16S sequences because of the divergent 16S

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.770
regions on these.

00:20:30.770 --> 00:20:36.810
Let's do another exercise with 16S.

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:46.750
What I have here if you want to follow along
there are three different 

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:51.750
sequences from some Bacillus species that
don't have species identification on them.

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:56.750
So we can try to find the best species match
for these against the 16S database.

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.060
I'm going to copy those.

00:20:59.060 --> 00:21:10.430
I can go back here to edit my search to get
to the blast page and I can Reset page to

00:21:10.430 --> 00:21:14.020
start over again.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:22.180
This is our first look at the rRNA/ITS databases.

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:27.900
I'll select the radio button for that and
select the top one that's the one we want

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:31.700
the 16S.

00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:35.300
Won't change any other parameters.

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:40.390
I can check the box for sequences from type
material just to make sure I'm looking at

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:49.310
the type strains here and run blast.

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:55.900
There are three sets of results for the three
different ones that we searched with and let's

00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:57.180
take a look at this.

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:01.570
We've got some potential matches here.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:06.600
One thing I want you to notice is, and this
is the point that I made earlier, there's

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:10.840
differences in the length of these sequences
that we have made.

00:22:10.840 --> 00:22:19.840
So what happens is, the best match which is
Bacillus drentensis, is lower down so what

00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:26.111
I want to do a lot of times as to make sure
I'm getting the best identity is to sort by

00:22:26.111 --> 00:22:28.260
percent identity.

00:22:28.260 --> 00:22:37.890
Then I can see that my best match is Bacillus
drentensis, and another one here for a different

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:39.300
strain of Bacillus drentensis.

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:45.970
The other thing you can do to sort of verify
things by look at the distance tree of results.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:48.540
We'll do that more than once today.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:55.460
Just to see how it fits in the tree.

00:22:55.460 --> 00:23:05.150
You can expand this node here and zoom into
see what's going on with that.

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:06.160
So here it is.

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:09.390
Fitting between those two Bacillus drentensis
sequences.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:13.121
So now do I know that this is the species?

00:23:13.121 --> 00:23:15.780
I don't know for sure.

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:21.810
It's close to that particular species.

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:25.760
I won't go to the other ones it's pretty much
the same exercise.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:32.900
And the reason this is happening is because
this NR_ sequence here is shorter than the

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:37.630
other ones and that's where we get a lower
query coverage even though the percent identity

00:23:37.630 --> 00:23:38.630
is higher.

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:42.690
So that is why matches might be dropped down
a little bit in your blast results.

00:23:42.690 --> 00:23:48.170
So moving on it.

00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:54.050
Let's move over to a fungal clone.

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:58.450
I'm going to retrieve it so you can see what
this is.

00:23:58.450 --> 00:24:04.059
I'm going to retrieve this from the Nucleotide
database.

00:24:04.059 --> 00:24:16.640
This is from a PopSet at NCBI where people
have submitted a whole bunch of sequences.

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:19.830
This is an uncultured soil fungus.

00:24:19.830 --> 00:24:26.170
There is a clone and we don't really know
what it is.

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:31.470
They have identified that it contains a part
of the 18S and internal transcribed spacer

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:35.260
region 1 and so on and so forth.

00:24:35.260 --> 00:24:38.320
So we can run a search with this.

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:52.070
By running a blast and picking one of our
rRNA/ITS databases but this time let's pick

00:24:52.070 --> 00:24:55.500
the ITS.

00:24:55.500 --> 00:25:11.020
This is a fungal sequence and we'll do a blast
search with that.

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:17.280
Again we have to make sure we are looking
at the best matches here so sort by percent

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:24.990
identity.

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:31.810
And if I do that my best match is Penicillium
subrubescens here.

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:34.870
Is this the species that were sure it is?

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:41.260
We cannot be sure but this is the best match
and we can take a look at the tree and see

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:47.490
if that confirms what were thinking about.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:26:04.070
It still comes out to be the closest thing
in this group of organsisms.

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:08.830
Those that I showed you work pretty well.

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:13.480
The last thing I want to do is fairly complicated
one involving several steps.

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:19.620
I want to point out to you on the last one
that basically there's some things you'll

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:20.620
need to think about.

00:26:20.620 --> 00:26:26.050
The database and the length of the query sequence
matter in terms of the identification that

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:27.220
you might get.

00:26:27.220 --> 00:26:36.400
So I'm going to go back to the handout.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:41.020
This is a fungal sequence that Barbara provided
to me.

00:26:41.020 --> 00:26:48.990
We can start out by doing a search against
a different database which is the default

00:26:48.990 --> 00:26:53.040
nucleotide database.

00:26:53.040 --> 00:27:00.000
I'll change my database to Standard nucleotide
databases here.

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.050
I'll keep my limit to Sequences from type
material on.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:20.290
Go ahead and run that.

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:22.490
For the moment let's just look at her best
matches.

00:27:22.490 --> 00:27:32.700
We have these Cortinarius species, ceskae
looks like it is the best match.

00:27:32.700 --> 00:27:37.450
Let's take a look at that match I want to
point something out to you.

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:41.320
If we take a look at the graphics link will
give us some information as to what it looks

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:42.320
like.

00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:49.870
One thing is, the sequence that we are dealing
with, the query sequence, incorporates more

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:52.460
than just the ITS regions.

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:59.290
So here's 28 S. It has a big chunk of the
28S in it so that's an important thing to

00:27:59.290 --> 00:28:02.080
keep in mind.

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:10.450
For example we could think about doing a search
against the 28S database.

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:16.660
Looking at the mismatches you've got mismatches
in the ITS regions but nothing within the

00:28:16.660 --> 00:28:17.960
5.8 or the 28S.

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:28.410
This does tell me that the query is longer
than just the ITS region.

00:28:28.410 --> 00:28:39.050
Let's go back and run this against the ITS
type strain database and see what that comes

00:28:39.050 --> 00:28:43.340
out like.

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:54.530
Change to ITS here on the blast form.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:29:08.050
We'll run this search.

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:14.020
We now have a different species at the top,
phoeniceus.

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:21.060
Notice that you can see the coverage is 71%
as pointed out before, the query sequence

00:29:21.060 --> 00:29:26.460
is longer than the sequence that we are matching.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:31.300
And ceskae which was number one before is
now number 3.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:36.809
Let's do the thing we did before and go to
the Distance Tree and see what comes up when

00:29:36.809 --> 00:29:39.700
that happens.

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:56.140
You can expand that and zoom to look at it.

00:29:56.140 --> 00:30:04.540
So now when we look at the distance tree results
the closest species is this one, cinnamomeus

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:10.330
and if you look up here you can find the other
ones in the tree, in different parts of the

00:30:10.330 --> 00:30:12.840
tree the ones we had before.

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:22.670
We now have a different closest match so let's
do one more thing which is to sort by percent

00:30:22.670 --> 00:30:29.370
identity.

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:33.920
Now we have croceus as our best match.

00:30:33.920 --> 00:30:39.040
That's the one that gives us the best percent
identity.

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:47.890
We've identified 4 different closest matches
and which one is it?

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:49.950
That's a good question.

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:53.970
In order to figure that out you might find
that you can't figure it out.

00:30:53.970 --> 00:31:02.950
There is a slide that Barbara showed me of
a phylogenetic analysis.

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:09.790
I'm going to switch back to the slides to
show you the results of that.

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:15.560
These are the species that we've looked at,
croceus, cinnamomeus, and phoeniceus.

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:18.380
They are closely related to each other.

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:26.880
It shows you where these things are and all
we can say is that it belongs to this species

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:27.880
group.

00:31:27.880 --> 00:31:31.040
We can't give a clear species identification.

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:37.500
So that's just a cautionary tale of the kind
of things you're going to run into using blast

00:31:37.500 --> 00:31:47.470
as a tool and the fact that we have a limited
taxonomic coverage for these organisms.

00:31:47.470 --> 00:31:52.679
We have about 10 minutes for questions.

00:31:52.679 --> 00:31:54.929
This slide shows you where you can get some
more information.

00:31:54.929 --> 00:31:58.820
These are our resources for learning about
things.

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:03.270
For questions you can write to our helpdesk,
info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:32:03.270 --> 00:32:07.050
And Rana are there any questions we can answer?

00:32:07.050 --> 00:32:11.230
[Rana Morris] Sure there are a lot of questions.

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:17.980
The first thing would like to talk about initially
are the targeted loci databases.

00:32:17.980 --> 00:32:23.460
There's a number of questions about what types
of sequences are actually located in that

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:26.510
particular database.

00:32:26.510 --> 00:32:31.350
One question was about what kind of sequences
are there.

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:36.175
One of the people on the webinar mentioned
about how there's many databases out there

00:32:36.175 --> 00:32:45.799
with different levels of quality of sequences
and what exactly goes into that targeted loci

00:32:45.799 --> 00:32:49.360
database and what is the level of curation.

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:54.850
Is it possible to expand these databases and
maybe fragment them into those that have good

00:32:54.850 --> 00:33:01.580
curation and those that have uncultured sequences
of from metagenomic profiling.

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:06.070
What goes into these targeted loci databases?

00:33:06.070 --> 00:33:15.549
[Peter Cooper] I think Barbara can answer
that question best.

00:33:15.549 --> 00:33:24.280
[Barbara Robbertse] For the RefSeq ITS database,
first we do a lot of sequence quality control

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:31.400
looking at non-ATG characters so there are
not too many of those.

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:39.380
We allow only 0.5% characters to be in our
dataset per sequence.

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:43.340
We look at the assembly.

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:47.929
Making sure correctly assembled.

00:33:47.929 --> 00:34:02.700
We use the ITSx program to make sure everything
is present and we also use this to check how

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.820
complete our sequences are.

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:14.350
Some of our sequences will have a flank on
the small subunit side and the large subunit

00:34:14.350 --> 00:34:18.659
side but it will never be longer than 100
bases.

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:26.929
So about 50% of the data set is actually complete
ITS1 to ITS2.

00:34:26.929 --> 00:34:36.810
Where they are partial we have generated a
statistic where we look for genus and what's

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:47.840
the expected length for ITS1 and for ITS2
and if we are covering 80% of either we allow

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:48.840
it to go in.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:57.420
Otherwise if we are too strict in housing
fully complete ITS one and two sequences then

00:34:57.420 --> 00:35:02.800
we will have too few copies of the fungal
kingdom.

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:04.130
Let's see what else.

00:35:04.130 --> 00:35:06.700
We make sure there's no bookkeeping errors.

00:35:06.700 --> 00:35:15.040
We check the sequence name association, and
we check that the sequences do not sit in

00:35:15.040 --> 00:35:19.190
weird places in the phylogenetic tree that
doesn't make sense in terms of its classification

00:35:19.190 --> 00:35:23.070
in the NCBI taxonomy.

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:31.730
[Peter] And I would say, Barbara you talked
about the fungal sequences, but the bacterial

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:35.090
sequences are curated in a similar way.

00:35:35.090 --> 00:35:42.340
The second part of the question was about
making available the uncultured sequences.

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:44.440
I don't know that we have any plans to do
that.

00:35:44.440 --> 00:35:48.020
There's certainly places you can get access
to those sequences.

00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:49.280
[Barbara] Yes.

00:35:49.280 --> 00:35:51.950
We don't have any plans to do that.

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:55.810
People can go to the UNITE database.

00:35:55.810 --> 00:36:05.830
They do sequence quality control and sequence
clustering and try to add species names.

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:07.320
[Peter] Thanks.

00:36:07.320 --> 00:36:13.700
Rana we are close to time but maybe you can
pick out one more question that would be a

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:14.740
popular one.

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:22.359
[Rana] There are a number that are popular
but let's go into the sequences.

00:36:22.359 --> 00:36:29.030
Looking for guidance on fungal identification
and also bacterial as well.

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:37.390
Is the ITS fungal identification specific
enough?

00:36:37.390 --> 00:36:46.690
Are there other genes that might be good to
include or would enhance the ability to identify

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:56.850
fungal sequences to the species level and
is it possible to have more than just one

00:36:56.850 --> 00:37:03.400
ITS and maybe another gene in order to do
the identification and the tree generation.

00:37:03.400 --> 00:37:09.340
[Barbara] Yes, so there are some secondary
barcodes that are important particularly for

00:37:09.340 --> 00:37:10.619
trichoderma and fusarium.

00:37:10.619 --> 00:37:18.780
One of those protein coding barcodes is called
elongation factor one and that can be used.

00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:20.470
In terms of other tools.

00:37:20.470 --> 00:37:27.710
Tree placement tools is probably the best
tool to use.

00:37:27.710 --> 00:37:30.130
There is a group called T-BAS.

00:37:30.130 --> 00:37:40.030
They maintain a reference tree you can use
your ITS or elongation factor sequences and

00:37:40.030 --> 00:37:46.200
search against the tree and it will have placement
of your sequence it will be shown highlighted

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:48.030
in the tree.

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:53.620
So your sequence is like fusarium, like trichoderma
whatever.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:38:00.270
I think they only do this for the filamentous
fungi.

00:38:00.270 --> 00:38:08.780
Other tools might be clustering tools but
I would stay away from it an use a tree placement

00:38:08.780 --> 00:38:10.050
tool.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:17.750
Other barcoding sequences you can use would
be RPB1 and RBP2 but I think the elongation

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:24.450
factor one is probably the best protein coding
gene to use after ITS.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:29.540
It depends on the plate or the genus you're
working with.

00:38:29.540 --> 00:38:37.540
I suggest doing some homework and if you're
working on a specific genus go and see what

00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:42.380
the literature says about genes other than
ITS which is the typical gene to use.

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:48.630
[Peter] We are essentially out of time so
I'm going to end the webinar.

00:38:48.630 --> 00:38:51.020
And Thanks Rana and Barbara.

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:52.789
Thanks everybody.

